<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Nassau</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Nassau is a German noble dynasty named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nassau-castle">Nassau Castle</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahn">Lahn</a>, which split in 1255 into a Walramian and an Ottonian line. The Ottonian branch produced the princes of Orange-Nassau, stadtholders and later kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, while the Walramian branch ruled the Duchy of Nassau until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed it in 1866. Under a family pact the Walramian Adolphe inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> in 1890, and his descendants are its grand dukes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau.jpg" alt="House of Nassau" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_Nassau.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The House of Nassau is a European dynasty named after a lordship associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nassau-castle">Nassau Castle</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>. Count Dudo-Henry of Laurenburg, who lived from about 1060 to about 1123, is considered its founder; he built Laurenburg Castle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahn">Lahn</a> around 1090. His son Rupert built Nassau Castle around 1125 and declared himself Count of Nassau, a title the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Worms did not officially acknowledge until 1159, under Rupert's son Walram. By then the county claimed rights of taxation, tolls and justice and can be considered a state. With the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>, Nassau emerged as an independent state within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1255 Henry II's sons Walram II and Otto I divided the Nassau possessions, creating the Walramian and Ottonian lines, a permanent division. The Walramian line concentrated on German lands. Adolf, count of Nassau from about 1276, was elected king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1292 and deposed by the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> in 1298, the first healthy ruler of the Empire deposed without a papal excommunication; he died at the Battle of Göllheim, fighting his successor Albert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>. In 1783 the heads of the branches sealed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nassau-family-pact">Nassau Family Pact</a> to regulate succession.</p><p>At the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> the Walramian branch had inherited or acquired all the Nassau ancestral lands and, with the permission of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>, proclaimed itself the Dukes of Nassau, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiesbaden">Wiesbaden</a>. The Duchy existed from 1806 as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>, and Adolph became its last reigning duke in August 1839, after the death of his father William. It was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1866 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a>, in which it had been Austria's ally.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Ottonian line established itself in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and its members became leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a>, stadtholders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> and eventually kings of the Netherlands. All <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourgish">Luxembourgish</a> monarchs since 1815 have been senior members of the House of Nassau, although the male lines of heirs became extinct in 1890 in the Netherlands and in 1912 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, and the thrones have since passed through the female line. By German tradition, which passes the name only in the male line, the house has been extinct since 1985, but neither Dutch nor Luxembourgish tradition and law consider it extinct. Today Nassau names a historical and cultural region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> with no political meaning.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Nassau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When William III died in 1890 leaving only a daughter, Wilhelmina, the Nassau Family Pact of 1783 passed Luxembourg to the other branch of the <b>House of Nassau</b>, and Adolphe, the former Duke of Nassau, became grand duke, giving the country its own dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
